## ELENA 7.0.2

- ELENA
  - [ADDED] Nested (named) classes
  - [ADDED] Nested extensions
  - [ADDED] Nested extensions template
  - [ADDED] inplace extension method
  - [ADDED] inplace extension template method
     
- ELC
  - [ADDED] a strict forward mapping mode (-xf), on by default : an unresolved forward aborts the compilation
  - [FIXED] multi-argument implicit lambda-function
  - [FIXED] critical error : initializing static fields
  - [FIXED] the external procedure frame offset was chosen by the host architecture instead of the target calling convention (amd64)
  - [FIXED] the PT_TLS program header was counted but never written, which left the interpreter entry empty (Linux, amd64)
  - [FIXED] critical error : an unresolved forward crashed the compiler instead of being reported

- VM

- RT
  - [FIXED] critical error : the stack was not 16-byte aligned when calling the GC routine, both in the STA and the MTA core (amd64, x32)
  - [FIXED] critical error : ExpandHeap / ExpandPerm never expanded the heap and always reported success (Linux, macOS)
  - [FIXED] full collect mode was ignored (Linux, macOS, amd64, aarch64, ppc64le)
  - [FIXED] system full collect passed the mode in the size register (amd64)
  - [FIXED] GC_ALLOCPERM did not follow the System V calling convention (amd64)
  - [FIXED] a failed mmap was not detected on 64 bit targets (Linux, macOS)
  - [FIXED] fill 3,0 core routine used wrong field offsets (amd64)
  - [FIXED] MTA : the thread table scan did not advance past a null slot, so every higher index thread kept running during a collection (amd64, x32)
  - [FIXED] MTA : the root scan walked a null frame chain and faulted while holding gc_lock (amd64, x32)
  - [FIXED] MTA : the safe point of GC_COLLECT returned to a path that requires gc_lock without retaking it (amd64, x32)
  - [FIXED] MTA : the collector did not hold gc_lock during the root scan and the collection (amd64, x32)
  - [FIXED] MTA : stopped threads waited on the personal event of the collecting thread, which the next collection resets (amd64, x32)
  - [FIXED] MTA : a newly started thread ran ELENA code while a collection was compacting (amd64, x32)
  - [FIXED] MTA : exclude did not order the safe region flag against gc_signal, so the collector waited for a thread that had already blocked (amd64, x32)
  - [FIXED] MTA : THREAD_WAIT clobbered the cached argument registers and acted on a stale gc_signal (amd64, x32)
  - [FIXED] MTA : the thread sync event was destroyed at thread exit while still published to the collector (Linux, macOS, Windows)
  - [FIXED] MTA : THREAD_WAIT published its own frame as the chain head and never restored the caller one, so the collector walked a chain living on dead stack (amd64, x32)
  - [FIXED] MTA : the table scan recognised the collecting thread through gc_signal instead of its own event, and ended up waiting for its own signal (amd64, x32)
  - [FIXED] MTA : a thread waiting on the barrier was still listed by the scan of the next collection (amd64, x32)
  - [FIXED] MTA : leaving a safe region was not serialised against the root scan (amd64, x32)
  - [FIXED] MTA : the thread frame chain was not initialised at thread registration, so a recycled thread block kept the frame pointer of the previous owner (Linux, macOS, Windows)
  - [FIXED] MTA : WaitForCollectionGCLA and SignalCollectionEndGCLA were not exported (macOS)
  - [FIXED] MTA : the GC critical section around thread creation and teardown was dropped by the JIT compiler
  - [FIXED] MTA : gc_signal was defined outside the GC table (x32)
  - [FIXED] MTA : the thread context was read through the Windows thread environment block on Linux (amd64)
  - [FIXED] MTA : the runtime calls of the collector passed their arguments in the Microsoft registers on Linux (amd64)
  - [FIXED] MTA : the program entry left the stack unaligned on Linux, so the first runtime call that spilled an SSE register faulted (amd64)
  - [FIXED] MTA : THREAD_WAIT signalled the collector with the wrong argument under the System V calling convention (amd64)
  - [FIXED] MTA : THREAD_WAIT kept the gc_lock address in a caller saved register across a runtime call (amd64)
  - [FIXED] MTA : THREAD_WAIT saved the object accumulator out of reach of the root scan, so it came back stale after a collection (amd64)
  - [FIXED] MTA : GC_ALLOCPERM left the stack unbalanced on the collect path and returned to the wrong address (amd64)
  - [FIXED] MTA : GC_COLLECT and GC_ALLOCPERM saved r12 and r13 inside the stack range handed to the root scan, where a raw heap address was collected as an object reference (amd64)
  - [FIXED] MTA : VEH_HANDLER assembled empty on Linux and shadowed the working handler of the single threaded core (amd64)
  - [FIXED] MTA : extopen and extclosen followed the Microsoft calling convention only, and never saved r14 (amd64)
  - [FIXED] MTA : the thread argument was read as a 32 bit value when creating a thread (amd64)
  - [FIXED] the object lock flag was never initialised, so a monitor taken on a recycled page never read free (amd64)
  - [FIXED] i386 shared libraries were compiled without -fPIC (Linux)

- SM

